      "clinician": "Dr. L. Bianchi",
      "specialty": "Cardiology",
      "reason": "Review of investigations",
      "note": "Reviewed the electrocardiogram of 2 March and the echocardiogram of 9 March with the patient. The echocardiogram is technically inadequate for the clinical question. Her symptoms have progressed further since February; she now stops three times on the same walk and has developed mild orthopnoea. NT-proBNP 1840 and a mildly raised high-sensitivity troponin support a cardiac cause. The differential is between an infiltrative cardiomyopathy, most importantly cardiac amyloidosis given her age and the septal appearance, and ischaemic disease that the paced rhythm prevents me from excluding electrocardiographically. Cardiac magnetic resonance with late gadolinium enhancement and T1 mapping is the study that distinguishes these, and the distinction determines management directly: a positive amyloid pattern would take her to bone scintigraphy and serum free light chains and, if confirmed, to disease-modifying therapy, whereas an ischaemic pattern of enhancement would take her to invasive angiography. I am requesting cardiac magnetic resonance with contrast on that basis. She has a permanent pacemaker; the device details will need to be confirmed with the implanting centre before scheduling.",
